1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,000
Hi. In this video, we'll look at how you can use Burp to test for clickjacking.

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:18,000
Clickjacking is a web security vulnerability that allows an attacker to trick users into clicking on hidden web page elements.

3
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:25,000
It's done by overlaying a disguised or invisible UI layer, usually using iframes, on top of a target web page,

4
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:29,000
fooling users into believing they're clicking something totally different.

5
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:36,000
For example, users may think they're entering a draw to win a luxury cruise. In reality, they're sending money to an attacker.

6
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:44,000
If you'd like to follow along, you can use the lab Basic Clickjacking with CSERF Token Protection from our Web Security Academy.

7
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:50,000
If you're using Burp Suite Professional, you can use Burp Scanner to test for clickjacking vulnerabilities.

8
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:52,000
So we'll take a look at this first.

9
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:57,000
Once the lab has launched, open your target web page in Burp's browser.

10
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:02,000
In this lab, we're going to use the My Account page of our fictional blog.

11
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:04,000
Go to the Proxy HTTP History tab.

12
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:07,000
Right-click the request you want to test.

13
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:10,000
And click Do Active Scan.

14
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:18,000
When the scan has finished, go to Burp's dashboard and review the Issue Activity log to identify any frameable response issues that the scanner found.

15
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:22,000
These indicate that your target web page is vulnerable to clickjacking attacks.

16
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:26,000
Now, we'll take a look at exploiting clickjacking vulnerabilities.

17
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:33,000
Although you can manually create a clickjacking proof of concept, this can be fairly tedious and time-consuming in practice.

18
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:37,000
When you're testing for clickjacking, we recommend using Burp's Click Bandit tool instead.

19
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:47,000
Once you've identified a vulnerable web page with an element that an attacker might target, open the top-level Burp menu, then click Burp Click Bandit.

20
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:51,000
Click Copy Click Bandit to clipboard to copy the script.

21
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:54,000
Load your target web page in Burp's browser.

22
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Paste the Click Bandit script into the Developer Tools console and the Click Bandit banner will appear.

23
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:03,000
Click Start.

24
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:07,000
This will reload the page within a frame, ready for the attack to be performed.

25
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:10,000
Click Bandit is now in active record mode.

26
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:19,000
Click Bandit records each of your clicks, so if you don't want to click to register at this stage, you can disable click actions from the checkbox on the Click Bandit banner.

27
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:23,000
This will help if a click will result in an action you don't want to happen.

28
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:27,000
As we'll be using Delete User as our potential target, we'll select this option.

29
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:31,000
Click the elements you've identified as potential targets for attackers.

30
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:35,000
Click Finish to end record mode and enter Review mode.

31
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:45,000
In Review mode, Click Bandit replays your click journey around the target's site with an attack UI overlaid, mimicking a real-world clickjacking attack.

32
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:50,000
You can now click the Attack UI buttons to verify each stage of the clickjacking attack.

33
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:53,000
But be aware, at this stage any clicks you make will register.

34
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:58,000
So if we click through the steps of this particular example, we would delete the user.

35
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:03,000
If you need to adjust the attack layer, you can adjust the zoom using the plus and minus.

36
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:07,000
Toggle Transparency to show or hide the page behind the attack layer.

37
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:11,000
Change the iframe position using the arrow keys on your keyboard.

38
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:18,000
And reset the attack layer to its original state, removing any changes you've made to its zoom or position.

39
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:24,000
Once you are happy with the settings, you can optionally choose to download the attack script as an HTML file.

